4. Meta‑kernel engines

4.1 MetaGenesisEngine

Inputs:

Current MetaKernelState

Simulation feedback metrics

Designer‑seeded constraints (e.g., “no total collapse”, “no unbounded energy growth”)

Outputs:

New LawGlyph candidates

Fused or mutated variants of existing laws

Core operations:

Generate: Synthesize new laws from templates + noise + constraints.

Mutate: Slightly alter parameters, scopes, or invariants.

Fuse: Combine compatible laws into composite glyphs.

Collapse: Reduce redundant or conflicting laws into simpler forms.

4.2 MetaEvaluationLoop

Responsibilities:

Run short simulation bursts under candidate law‑sets.

Score candidates on:

Stability

Expressiveness

Novelty

Compliance with anchors

Result:

Rank candidates.

Mark some as PROMOTABLE, others as REJECTED, others as ARCHIVE_ONLY.

4.3 MetaPromotionProtocol

Rules:

Only promote if:

Anchors remain satisfied.

Rewrite window limits are respected.

On promotion:

Update active_lawset.

Seal previous state into a Capsule (encrypted, signed).

Append to AuditTrail.

5. Encryption model

5.1 Key hierarchy

Root Designer Key:CM-BLACKWELL-ROOT — offline, used only to sign subkeys.

Blueprint Key:Encrypts meta‑kernel blueprints and law glyph definitions.

Simulation State Key:Encrypts runtime states, timelines, and organism configurations.

Audit Key:Encrypts audit logs and capsule archives.

5.2 Encryption policies

Blueprints:

Always encrypted at rest.

Decrypted only inside a trusted execution context.

Simulation states:

Optionally stream‑encrypted during runtime.

Snapshots always encrypted and capsule‑sealed.

Meta‑kernel transitions:

Every promotion event produces:

Encrypted Capsule of old state.

Signed record:C MATTHEW BLACKWELL INDEPENDENT DESIGNER :: META-TRANSITION #N.

